%U A213892 1051,1087,1103,1123,1163,1259,1279,1307,1319,1399,1423,1451,1471
%N A213892 Fixed points of a sequence h(n) defined by the minimum number of 3's in the relation n*[n,3,3,...,3,n] = [x,...,x] between simple continued fractions.
%C A213892 In a variant of A213891, multiply n by a number with simple continued fraction [n,3,3,...,3,n] and increase the number of 3's until the continued fraction of the product has the same first and last entry (called x in the NAME).
%C A213892 Examples are
%C A213892   2 * [2, 3, 3, 2] = [4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 4],
%C A213892   3 * [3, 3, 3] = [9, 1, 9],
%C A213892   4 * [4, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 4] = [17, 4, 1, 2, 1, 4, 17],
%C A213892   5 * [5, 3, 3, 5] = [26, 1, 1, 26],
%C A213892   6 * [6, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 6] = [37, 1, 4, 2, 4, 1, 37],
%C A213892   7 * [7, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 7] = [51, 8, 2, 1, 2, 8, 51].
%C A213892 The number of 3's needed defines the sequence h(n) = 2, 1, 5, 2, 5, 7, 5, 5, 2, ... (n>=2).
%C A213892 The current sequence contains the fixed points of h, i.e., those n where h(n)=n.
%C A213892 We conjecture that this sequence contains prime numbers analogous to the sequence of prime numbers A000057, in the sense that, instead of referring to the Fibonacci sequences (sequences satisfying f(n) = f(n-1) + f(n-2) with arbitrary positive integer values for f(1) and f(2)) it refers to the sequences satisfying f(n) = 3*f(n-1) + f(n-2), A006190, A003688, A052924, etc. This would mean that a prime is in the sequence if and only if it divides some term in each of the sequences satisfying f(n) = 3*f(n-1) + f(n-2).
%C A213892 The above sequence h() is recorded as A262213. - _M. F. Hasler_, Sep 15 2015
%C A213892 From _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Aug 04 2026: (Start)
%C A213892 In response to the conjecture above, an exhaustive computation using the rank criterion in the Formula section found no composite term n<=10^6. The proposed characterization of the prime terms by analogy with A000057 is exact, as explained below.
%C A213892 For a prime p, write B(k)=A006190(k) and define T([u:v])=[3*u+v:u] on P^1(F_p), equivalently T(x)=3+1/x. Since T^k(infinity)=[B(k+1):B(k)] for k>=0, A322907(p) is the first return time of infinity. Thus p is a term if and only if T is a single cycle of length p+1 on P^1(F_p). Equivalently, the p+1 consecutive coefficient ratios [B(k+1):B(k)], 0<=k<=p, of x/(1-3*x-x^2)=Sum_{k>=0} B(k)*x^k enumerate every point of P^1(F_p) exactly once.
%C A213892 For an odd prime p, the condition is also equivalent to X^2-3*X-1 being projectively primitive over F_p, or to (3+sqrt(13))/(3-sqrt(13)) having order p+1 in the norm-one subgroup of GF(p^2)^*. Consequently, the Legendre symbol (13/p) is -1 and p == 3 (mod 4). Hence every odd prime term is congruent to 7, 11, 15, 19, 31, or 47 (mod 52).
%C A213892 For a prime p, these conditions are also equivalent to p dividing a term of every integer sequence satisfying f(k+2)=3*f(k+1)+f(k). Thus the equivalence proposed in the conjecture above holds for primes. (End)
%C A213892 In fact, every term is prime: the Formula section gives A322907(k)=k+1 for every term, and Lehmer's maximal-rank theorem, applied to A006190, implies that k is prime; see A214028. Hence the divisibility characterization above holds for every term, not only for primes. See A213891 for the Silver analog. - _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Aug 23 2026
%F A213892 For n>=2, A262213(n) = min{m>=1: n | A006190(m+1)} = A322907(n)-1. Therefore k is a term of this sequence if and only if A322907(k) = k+1. Equivalently, k is a term if and only if A006190(k+1) == 0 (mod k) and A006190((k+1)/q) != 0 (mod k) for every prime divisor q of k+1. - _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Aug 04 2026
%e A213892 The example k=7 above has A262213(7)=7, so 7 is a term. Equivalently, A322907(7)=8=7+1. Under the map T(x)=3+1/x modulo 7, this gives the complete orbit infinity -> 3 -> 1 -> 4 -> 5 -> 6 -> 2 -> 0 -> infinity on P^1(F_7). By contrast, A262213(11)=3, equivalently A322907(11)=4 != 12, so 11 is not a term. - _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Aug 04 2026
%t A213892 f[m_, n_] := Block[{c, k = 1}, c[x_, y_] := ContinuedFraction[x FromContinuedFraction[Join[{x}, Table[m, {y}], {x}]]]; While[First@ c[n, k] != Last@ c[n, k], k++]; k]; Select[Range[2, 1000], f[3, #] == # &] (* _Michael De Vlieger_, Sep 16 2015 *)
%o A213892 (PARI)
%o A213892 {a(n) = local(t, m=1); if( n<2, 0, while( 1,
%o A213892    t = contfracpnqn( concat([n, vector(m,i,3), n]));
%o A213892    t = contfrac(n*t[1,1]/t[2,1]);
%o A213892    if(t[1]<n^2 || t[#t]<n^2, m++, break));
%o A213892 m)};
%o A213892 for(k=1,1500,if(k==a(k),print1(a(k),", ")));
%o A213892 (PARI)
%o A213892 {zBproj(n)=my(u=1, v=0, t, k=0); while(1, t=u; u=(3*u+v)%n; v=t; k++; if(v==0, return(k)))};
%o A213892 for(n=2, 1500, if(zBproj(n)==n+1, print1(n, ", ")));  \\ _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Aug 29 2026
